{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UUb6G7iYGJhF5EJiPjqnx8","type":"tool_result","content":"A/B tests on social networks and marketplaces violate the no-interference assumption: whether a user's outcome changes depends on who else is treated. The platform's decision-relevant estimand is then the \\emph{global average treatment effect} (GATE), the mean over units of the difference between the outcome when all units are treated and when all are untreated. The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased for the GATE under interference \\citep{Eckles2016Design,Ugander2013GraphCluster}, and design-based alternatives such as Horvitz--Thompson (HT) estimators of neighbourhood exposure \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Ugander2013GraphCluster} avoid modelling assumptions at the price of large variance when the all-treated and all-control neighbourhood events are rare.\n\nA model-based alternative is \\emph{regression-based GATE estimation} \\citep{Chin2018RegressionAF}: fit a regression of outcomes on the unit's own treatment, a summary of its neighbours' treatments (an \\emph{exposure mapping}) and covariates; predict every unit's outcome under the all-treated and all-control assignments; and average the difference. This is attractive because it uses every unit rather than only those with extreme neighbourhoods. Its weakness is also visible in the construction: the two prediction points are the corners of exposure space (neighbour-treated fraction 1 and 0), which a randomized experiment with a moderate treatment probability rarely visits, so the estimate leans on the functional form of the fitted model. How accurate such estimators are in practice, which features of the mechanism, graph and design drive their error, and when they fail has not been laid out in a controlled grid.\n\n\\paragraph{Question and thesis.} We ask: in a single randomized network experiment, how accurately does a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which properties of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design determine when it works? The measured pattern is that per-case error is low in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction and higher in the three cases whose form is not, with no visible degradation across graph family, interference strength (up to three times larger) or cluster-versus-Bernoulli assignment within the tested range, and that the model-based intervals undercover. A natural hypothesis, which the interference literature raises under the heading of misspecified exposure mappings and interference of unknown form \\citep{Savje2023Misspecified,Savje2021Unknown}, is that accuracy depends on how well the fitted exposure mapping matches the generating mechanism. We state this as a hypothesis: the grid is not factorial, and the mapping the estimator selected in each case was not recorded in verified form.\n\n\\paragraph{What we do.} We evaluate one frozen estimator, a BIC-gated library of parametric exposure mappings with linear regression as the fallback, together with four reference estimators (difference-in-means, plain linear exposure regression, a neighbourhood HT estimator and an oracle regression using the true mapping) on a fixed eight-case grid, using 24 seeds that were not used for model selection. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes the pipeline and the grid.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A transparent, scale-free protocol (relative RMSE per case, averaged over cases) with four reference estimators and three negative controls. (ii) Measured accuracy of regression-based GATE estimation across five interference forms, three graph families and two assignment designs, with single-factor case contrasts that are consistent with, but do not establish, exposure-mapping misspecification as the main error source. (iii) Negative results: nominal 95\\% intervals cover 0.839 of the time, the two-hop case stays at relative RMSE 0.452 and its cause is unresolved, and two other estimator classes (a flexible ridge ensemble and a linear model with a design-weighted residual correction) scored worse than the gated library on the model-selection seeds. We make no claim of a bound, a theorem, or superiority beyond this grid; Section~\\ref{sec:limits} lists what the evidence cannot support.\n\n\\subsection{Reliability: seed variability and interval coverage}\n\\label{sec:reliability}\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} On this grid, the frozen regression GATE estimator is accurate (relative RMSE 0.031--0.054 per case at $n=3000$) in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour fraction, across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. In the saturating, count$\\times$degree and two-hop cases the per-case error is 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452. Whether this reflects exposure-mapping misspecification, as the literature on misspecified mappings would suggest, is a hypothesis: we did not verify which mapping was selected in any case, and case 6 shows that the library's containing the true form does not guarantee a low error. We do not extrapolate beyond the tested regime. The choice of gate margin and library members is also a researcher degree of freedom that we did not ablate.\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the HT reference scored a mean relative RMSE of 0.994 (Table~\\ref{tab:agg}); we did not diagnose the cause (variance, sparsity of fully exposed neighbourhoods, or the error cap), and regression errors were larger in cases 6--8 without our having verified the cause. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_ht_rmse_rel]]\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so the gain over linear regression may partly reflect the library containing shapes close to the grid's mechanisms; this was not tested, and no ablation or per-case gate logging exists. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression. Relative error divides by $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and is capped at 2, so it treats heavy-tailed errors differently from absolute error.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was developed after the two earlier estimator classes had been scored on the model-selection seeds; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by per-case intervals, and several pairs vary more than one property. (4) Per-case values for the baselines, the bias, and the coverage are unavailable, so we make no per-case baseline comparison. (5) The cause of the larger errors in cases 6--8 (including the two-hop failure) and of the undercoverage is not established, and the mapping selected per case was not verified, so the mapping-mismatch account is a hypothesis. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds, and their per-case results are not used. (7) All mechanisms are synthetic; we ran no experiment on public graphs (e.g., Facebook100), real outcomes, learned graph representations, or other designs. (8) Qualification of the final evaluation is a statement about the measured result on this grid.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe measured how well a regression-based GATE estimator recovers the truth under network interference on an eight-case synthetic grid. Relative RMSE was 0.031--0.054 per case in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction, regardless of graph family, a threefold increase in interference strength, or cluster versus Bernoulli assignment, and 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452 in the saturating, count-by-degree and two-hop cases. A BIC-gated library of parametric mappings lowered mean relative RMSE to 0.126 from 0.180 for plain linear regression, but remained 2.8 times above an oracle given the true mapping, did not fix the two-hop case, and produced intervals that cover only 0.839 of the time. We hypothesize that exposure-mapping mismatch and extrapolation to the all-treated and all-control corners drive the larger errors; this account is consistent with the single-factor contrasts but untested, because the grid is not factorial and the selected mappings were not verified. These findings are limited to one size, one treatment probability, and shared case specifications between model selection and final scoring. Next steps are a factorial extension with per-case intervals, a gate and library ablation with logged mapping choices, two-hop diagnostics, calibrated intervals, and public-graph experiments.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f8976b66-d35d-4fa4-b183-fd061f99b7f5","uuid":"4eb73280-fe0b-4fb3-b682-7dc614c56b79","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:05:09.297Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"A/B tests on social networks and marketplaces violate the no-interference assumption: whether a user's outcome changes depends on who else is treated. The platform's decision-relevant estimand is then the \\emph{global average treatment effect} (GATE), the mean over units of the difference between the outcome when all units are treated and when all are untreated. The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased for the GATE under interference \\citep{Eckles2016Design,Ugander2013GraphCluster}, and design-based alternatives such as Horvitz--Thompson (HT) estimators of neighbourhood exposure \\citep{Aronow2013EstimatingAC,Ugander2013GraphCluster} avoid modelling assumptions at the price of large variance when the all-treated and all-control neighbourhood events are rare.\n\nA model-based alternative is \\emph{regression-based GATE estimation} \\citep{Chin2018RegressionAF}: fit a regression of outcomes on the unit's own treatment, a summary of its neighbours' treatments (an \\emph{exposure mapping}) and covariates; predict every unit's outcome under the all-treated and all-control assignments; and average the difference. This is attractive because it uses every unit rather than only those with extreme neighbourhoods. Its weakness is also visible in the construction: the two prediction points are the corners of exposure space (neighbour-treated fraction 1 and 0), which a randomized experiment with a moderate treatment probability rarely visits, so the estimate leans on the functional form of the fitted model. How accurate such estimators are in practice, which features of the mechanism, graph and design drive their error, and when they fail has not been laid out in a controlled grid.\n\n\\paragraph{Question and thesis.} We ask: in a single randomized network experiment, how accurately does a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which properties of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design determine when it works? The measured pattern is that per-case error is low in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction and higher in the three cases whose form is not, with no visible degradation across graph family, interference strength (up to three times larger) or cluster-versus-Bernoulli assignment within the tested range, and that the model-based intervals undercover. A natural hypothesis, which the interference literature raises under the heading of misspecified exposure mappings and interference of unknown form \\citep{Savje2023Misspecified,Savje2021Unknown}, is that accuracy depends on how well the fitted exposure mapping matches the generating mechanism. We state this as a hypothesis: the grid is not factorial, and the mapping the estimator selected in each case was not recorded in verified form.\n\n\\paragraph{What we do.} We evaluate one frozen estimator, a BIC-gated library of parametric exposure mappings with linear regression as the fallback, together with four reference estimators (difference-in-means, plain linear exposure regression, a neighbourhood HT estimator and an oracle regression using the true mapping) on a fixed eight-case grid, using 24 seeds that were not used for model selection. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes the pipeline and the grid.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) A transparent, scale-free protocol (relative RMSE per case, averaged over cases) with four reference estimators and three negative controls. (ii) Measured accuracy of regression-based GATE estimation across five interference forms, three graph families and two assignment designs, with single-factor case contrasts that are consistent with, but do not establish, exposure-mapping misspecification as the main error source. (iii) Negative results: nominal 95\\% intervals cover 0.839 of the time, the two-hop case stays at relative RMSE 0.452 and its cause is unresolved, and two other estimator classes (a flexible ridge ensemble and a linear model with a design-weighted residual correction) scored worse than the gated library on the model-selection seeds. We make no claim of a bound, a theorem, or superiority beyond this grid; Section~\\ref{sec:limits} lists what the evidence cannot support.\n\n\\subsection{Reliability: seed variability and interval coverage}\n\\label{sec:reliability}\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} On this grid, the frozen regression GATE estimator is accurate (relative RMSE 0.031--0.054 per case at $n=3000$) in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour fraction, across the three graph families, a threefold change in interference strength and both assignment designs. In the saturating, count$\\times$degree and two-hop cases the per-case error is 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452. Whether this reflects exposure-mapping misspecification, as the literature on misspecified mappings would suggest, is a hypothesis: we did not verify which mapping was selected in any case, and case 6 shows that the library's containing the true form does not guarantee a low error. We do not extrapolate beyond the tested regime. The choice of gate margin and library members is also a researcher degree of freedom that we did not ablate.\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to design-based estimation.} The HT reference is design-based, whereas the regression estimators accept model risk in exchange for using every unit. In this grid at $n=3000$ the HT reference scored a mean relative RMSE of 0.994 (Table~\\ref{tab:agg}); we did not diagnose the cause (variance, sparsity of fully exposed neighbourhoods, or the error cap), and regression errors were larger in cases 6--8 without our having verified the cause. This does not imply that regression dominates design-based approaches: we compared analyses under two fixed designs, did not tune HT (for instance with exposure thresholds as in \\citealp{Thiyageswaran2024DataAdaptive}), and did not vary graph density or size. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.baseline_ht_rmse_rel]]\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} The library includes the exact case-6 sigmoid and saturating and count-based shapes close to the grid's other forms, so the gain over linear regression may partly reflect the library containing shapes close to the grid's mechanisms; this was not tested, and no ablation or per-case gate logging exists. A practitioner whose true mechanism lies outside the library would not be protected by the gate; the fallback is then plain linear regression. Relative error divides by $|\\gate|\\ge0.5$ and is capped at 2, so it treats heavy-tailed errors differently from absolute error.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Case specifications are shared between model selection and final scoring, and the library was developed after the two earlier estimator classes had been scored on the model-selection seeds; the final result is out-of-sample over seeds but not over mechanisms. (2) There is a single graph size ($n=3000$) and a single assignment probability (0.5). (3) Contrasts are single case pairs, not a factorial design; they are not accompanied by per-case intervals, and several pairs vary more than one property. (4) Per-case values for the baselines, the bias, and the coverage are unavailable, so we make no per-case baseline comparison. (5) The cause of the larger errors in cases 6--8 (including the two-hop failure) and of the undercoverage is not established, and the mapping selected per case was not verified, so the mapping-mismatch account is a hypothesis. (6) There was no ablation of the gate margin or library components, so we cannot attribute the gain over linear regression to any particular mapping or to the gate itself; only three estimator classes were scored once on the model-selection seeds, and their per-case results are not used. (7) All mechanisms are synthetic; we ran no experiment on public graphs (e.g., Facebook100), real outcomes, learned graph representations, or other designs. (8) Qualification of the final evaluation is a statement about the measured result on this grid.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe measured how well a regression-based GATE estimator recovers the truth under network interference on an eight-case synthetic grid. Relative RMSE was 0.031--0.054 per case in the five cases whose generating form is linear in the neighbour-treated fraction, regardless of graph family, a threefold increase in interference strength, or cluster versus Bernoulli assignment, and 0.198, 0.151 and 0.452 in the saturating, count-by-degree and two-hop cases. A BIC-gated library of parametric mappings lowered mean relative RMSE to 0.126 from 0.180 for plain linear regression, but remained 2.8 times above an oracle given the true mapping, did not fix the two-hop case, and produced intervals that cover only 0.839 of the time. We hypothesize that exposure-mapping mismatch and extrapolation to the all-treated and all-control corners drive the larger errors; this account is consistent with the single-factor contrasts but untested, because the grid is not factorial and the selected mappings were not verified. These findings are limited to one size, one treatment probability, and shared case specifications between model selection and final scoring. Next steps are a factorial extension with per-case intervals, a gate and library ablation with logged mapping choices, two-hop diagnostics, calibrated intervals, and public-graph experiments.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
